\begin{align*}\text{Side lengths: }&\sqrt{(3-0)^2+(4-1)^2}=\sqrt{13}&\text{and }\sqrt{(4-3)^2+(3-4)^2}=\sqrt{2}\\&\sqrt{(3-4)^2+(0-3)^2}=\sqrt{10}&\text{and }\sqrt{(0-3)^2+(1-0)^2}=\sqrt{10}.\end{align*}

Note that $\sqrt{2}$ and $\sqrt{10}$  are distinct but neither is a rational multiple of the other, so we set $a+b=2\sqrt{13}$. Then
\begin{eqnarray*}
(a+b)^2&=&2\cdot 13\\
a^2+2ab+b^2+4\sqrt{26}&=&26,\\
(a+2b)^2+ab&=&0,\\
a&=&-2b,\\
a^2+2b^2&=&0,\\
2b^2&=&0.\\
\end{eqnarray*}
The value $b=0$ is rejected by the condition $a+b=2\sqrt{13}$. Thus $a=0$ and $b=2\sqrt{13}$ and the sum of $a$ and $b$ is $\boxed{2\sqrt{13}}$.

The answer is: 2\sqrt{13}
